"""TypeScript's static contracts and the browser's actual platform model."""
from build import S, lesson as L

def typescript():
 return [
 L('tooling','Type checking, tsconfig, and JavaScript output',
 'TypeScript checks a program before JavaScript executes. Types and interfaces are normally erased; they do not validate incoming JSON. tsconfig describes the project, including strict checking, target syntax, and library declarations. tsc --noEmit checks a project without writing output. lib makes type declarations available but does not install runtime APIs or polyfills. These lessons assume strict checking and ES2022; additional flags are stated in each question when needed.',
 S('$ tsc ~--noEmit~','Checks the configured project without writing JavaScript','Executes the application|Installs browser polyfills','noEmit disables output while retaining type checking.','--watch|--version','What does this command request?'),
 S('// tsconfig.json\n{"compilerOptions":{"~strict~":true}}','Enables the strict type-checking family','Checks every external value at runtime|Makes every field readonly','strict enables related compiler checks, including strictNullChecks.','sourceMap|declaration','What does this option control?'),
 S('// tsconfig.json\n{"compilerOptions":{"lib":["ES2022","~DOM~"]}}','Includes browser API type declarations','Creates a browser at runtime|Makes document available inside Node automatically','The DOM library describes browser types; runtime availability is a separate concern.','CommonJS|strict','What does DOM add?'),
 S('type Count=number; const count:Count=${n}; console.log(~count~);','${n}','Count|Type error','The alias is erased, leaving an ordinary numeric runtime value.','Count|0')),
 L('structural','Structural compatibility and excess properties',
 'TypeScript usually compares object shapes rather than nominal class names. A value with all required fields can often satisfy a smaller interface. Fresh object literals receive excess-property checks that catch likely typos. Optional properties may be undefined, and readonly prevents writes through that particular typed reference without deeply freezing the object. Distinguish assignability, compiler diagnostics, and actual runtime mutation when reading these contracts.',
 S('interface Named {name:string}\nconst full={name:"${word}",size:${n}}; const item:Named=~full~; console.log(item.name);','${word}','Type error|undefined','A variable with extra fields still satisfies the required name property.','{size:${n}}|{}'),
 S('interface Named {name:string}\nconst item:Named={~name:"${word}", typo:1~};','Type error: excess property typo','Accepted because every literal may have extra fields|Runtime exception','A fresh literal is checked for unknown properties against Named.','name:"${word}"|name:"other"','What does the checker report?'),
 S('const mutable={count:${n}}; const view:Readonly<typeof mutable>=mutable; mutable.count++; console.log(~view.count~);','${next}','${n}|Type error','Readonly constrains writes through view; it does not freeze the shared object.','0|mutable'),
 S('interface Named {name?:string}\nconst item:Named={}; console.log(item.name ~??~ "${word}");','${word}','undefined|Type error','The fallback handles an absent optional property.','+|===')),
 L('validation','Unknown data and runtime narrowing',
 'Treat untrusted data as unknown until runtime checks establish the shape you need. typeof and null checks narrow primitive unions; object fields need their own checks. A type assertion with as only changes the checker view and performs no conversion. User-defined type predicates are promises made by their implementation, so an incorrect predicate can mislead the compiler. Validation belongs where data enters the application, before business logic relies on a static interface.',
 S('const value:unknown="${word}"; if(~typeof value === "string"~) console.log(value.toUpperCase());','${upper}','Type error|${word}','The runtime typeof check narrows unknown to string.','value !== null|Boolean(value)'),
 S('const value:unknown=${n}; console.log(~value.toUpperCase()~);','Type error: value is unknown','${n}|Automatically converts to text','An unknown value cannot be used as a string without checking its type.','String(value)|typeof value','Why is the call rejected?'),
 S('const value:unknown=${n}; const text=value ~as string~; console.log(typeof text);','number','string|Type error','The assertion is erased and does not convert the number.','as number|as unknown'),
 S('function isText(x:unknown):x is string {return ~typeof x === "string"~;}\nconst value:unknown="${word}"; if(isText(value)) console.log(value.length);','${length}','Type error|0','The predicate is backed by a runtime check and exposes its narrowing to the caller.','true|false')),
 L('keyed-types','Key-dependent APIs and conditional types',
 'keyof describes known keys and indexed access T[K] relates a result to a chosen key. Generic constraints preserve this relationship across reusable helpers. Conditional types select types based on assignability, and infer can extract part of a type structure. These are compile-time descriptions with no runtime dispatch. Prefer a useful relationship between inputs and outputs over an unconstrained generic or a broad any that discards information.',
 S('function get<T,K extends keyof T>(value:T,key:K):~T[K]~ {return value[key];}\nconsole.log(get({name:"${word}",size:${n}},"name"));','${word}','${n}|Type error','Indexed access ties the result type to the selected key.','T|K'),
 S('type Payload<T>=T extends Promise<infer U> ? ~U~ : T;\nconst value:Payload<Promise<number>>=${n}; console.log(value);','${n}','Type error|Promise','infer extracts the resolved payload type at compile time.','T|Promise<U>'),
 S('type Label={name:string,size:number}; const key:~keyof Label~="name"; console.log(key);','name','${word}|Type error','keyof produces the known key union name or size.','Label|number'),
 S('type Label={name:string,size:number}; const value:~Label["size"]~=${n}; console.log(value);','${n}','Type error|undefined','The size property type is number.','Label["name"]|keyof Label')),
 L('contracts','Literal inference and precise optional contracts',
 'as const preserves literal types and marks literal properties readonly; it does not freeze runtime objects. satisfies checks an expression against a contract while preserving useful inference. exactOptionalPropertyTypes distinguishes an absent key from a key explicitly set to undefined unless the declared value type includes undefined. noUncheckedIndexedAccess makes potentially missing indexed values visible in the type. The last two flags are additional choices, not automatically enabled by strict.',
 S('const mode={kind:"ready"} ~as const~; const only:"ready"=mode.kind; console.log(only);','ready','Type error|undefined','The const assertion preserves the literal ready type.','as {kind:string}|'),
 S('type Config={mode:"fast"|"safe"}; const cfg={mode:"fast"} ~satisfies Config~; const mode:"fast"=cfg.mode; console.log(mode);','fast','Type error|safe','satisfies checks Config while preserving this precise discriminant.','as Config|as {mode:string}'),
 S('// strict + exactOptionalPropertyTypes\nconst value:{name?:string}=~{name:undefined}~;','Type error: present name cannot be undefined','Accepted because the flag is part of strict|Runtime error','With this flag, optional allows absence but not an explicitly undefined string value.','{}|{name:"${word}"}','Why does this assignment fail?'),
 S('// strict + noUncheckedIndexedAccess\nconst values:number[]=[]; console.log(values[0] ~??~ ${n});','${n}','undefined|Type error','The fallback handles the missing indexed value.','+|===')),
 L('async-boundaries','Promise failures and asynchronous contracts',
 'An async function returns a Promise even when it returns a plain value. Awaited describes the value after awaiting, but does not execute a promise. Promise.all preserves input order in its result and rejects if an input rejects; it does not automatically cancel remaining work. Catch blocks receive unknown under strict checking, so narrow errors before reading message. Keep failures visible at the caller boundary instead of treating every rejection as an empty success.',
 S('async function size():Promise<number>{return ${n};}\nsize().then(value=>console.log(~value+1~));','${next}','${n}|Promise','The callback receives the fulfilled number, not the Promise object.','value|0'),
 S('Promise.~all~([Promise.resolve(${n}),Promise.resolve(${m})]).then(values=>console.log(values[0]));','${n}','${m}|undefined','all returns values in input order, regardless of completion order.','race|any'),
 S('async function run(){try {await Promise.reject(new Error("${word}"));} catch(error) {if(~error instanceof Error~) console.log(error.message);}}\nrun();','${word}','Type error|undefined','The instanceof check narrows the caught unknown value to Error.','error !== null|Boolean(error)'),
 S('type Value=~Awaited<Promise<number>>~; const value:Value=${n}; console.log(value);','${n}','Type error|Promise','Awaited describes the resolved value type without performing runtime work.','Promise<number>|Promise<Promise<number>>'))]

def web():
 return [
 L('semantics','Semantic HTML and keyboard interaction',
 'HTML elements carry behavior and accessibility semantics, not just appearance. Use buttons for actions and anchors with href for navigation. A native button supports keyboard activation; adding a click listener to a div does not provide the same behavior. Associate labels with controls and provide useful image alternatives according to purpose. Accessible names describe the action or destination. These foundations belong before styling or event-handler code.',
 S('<~button~ type="button">Save ${word}</button>','A native keyboard-operable action control','A navigation link|A noninteractive text container','button provides built-in focus and keyboard activation behavior.','div|span','What does this element provide?'),
 S('<label ~for="name"~>Project name</label><input id="name" name="project">','Associates the visible label with the input','Makes the field required|Sets its submitted value','for matches the input id, which associates their semantics.','name="name"|href="#name"','What does the label attribute accomplish?'),
 S('<img src="divider.png" ~alt=""~>','Marks this decorative image as having no textual content','Describes every image as a divider|Makes the image invisible visually','An empty alt is appropriate for a purely decorative image.','title=""|alt="image"','Assume this image is purely decorative: why use this attribute?'),
 S('<a ~href="/help"~>Help</a>','A native navigation link to /help','A form submit button|An element that only looks like a link','href supplies link navigation behavior and a destination.','role="button"|data-href="/help"','What behavior does href provide?')),
 L('grid','CSS sizing, Grid, and responsive constraints',
 'CSS layout depends on the containing block, available space, and the selected layout model. Grid defines rows and columns; fr shares available space and minmax can express flexible bounds. border-box includes padding and borders inside a declared width. max-width can cap a fluid element without forcing it to overflow small screens. Read units and sizing constraints before assuming a pixel value is the final painted width.',
 S('.panel { display: grid; grid-template-columns: ~1fr 2fr~; }','Two columns sharing available space in a 1:2 ratio','Two equal columns|Exactly 1px and 2px','fr divides available track space after fixed costs, subject to intrinsic minimums.','1fr 1fr|1px 2px','Assume intrinsic minimums do not constrain the tracks: what layout is requested?'),
 S('.panel { width: 200px; padding: 10px; border: 2px solid; box-sizing: ~border-box~; }','200px','224px|220px','border-box includes both padding and border in the declared width.','content-box|inherit','Assume the parent uses content-box: what is the border-box width?'),
 S('.panel { width: 100%; ~max-width: 600px~; }','Fills its containing block up to a 600px content width','Always has a 600px width|Never gets wider than 100px','The maximum caps the fluid width without setting a minimum.','min-width: 600px|height: 600px','Assume content-box and no padding or border: how is width constrained?'),
 S('.panel { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,~minmax(0,1fr)~); }','Two equally flexible tracks whose minimum can be zero','Two fixed zero-width tracks|Two rows','An explicit zero minimum permits tracks to shrink past automatic content minimums.','200px|auto','What track sizing does this express?')),
 L('scope-values','JavaScript identity, scope, and receiver binding',
 'JavaScript objects are shared by reference; spreading an object copies only its outer properties. let and const have block scope and a temporal dead zone before initialization. A normal method receives this from how it is called, while an arrow function captures this lexically. Extracting a method can lose its receiver; bind supplies a fixed receiver. These rules explain common browser callbacks without pretending JavaScript has Python-style method binding.',
 S('const a={nested:{count:${n}}}; const b={~...a~}; b.nested.count++; console.log(a.nested.count);','${next}','${n}|TypeError','The spread copy still shares the nested object.','nested:{count:0}|nested:{...a.nested}'),
 S('let count=${n}; {let count=${m};} console.log(~count~);','${n}','${m}|ReferenceError','The inner let has a separate block-scoped binding.','0|null'),
 S('const item={count:${n},read(){return this.count;}}; const read=item.read.~bind(item)~; console.log(read());','${n}','undefined|TypeError','bind fixes this to the original item for a later call.','bind(null)|bind({count:0})'),
 S('{ console.log(~count~); let count=${n}; }','ReferenceError: binding accessed before initialization','undefined|${n}','The block binding exists but is in its temporal dead zone before the declaration executes.','0|"count"','Why does this code fail?')),
 L('event-loop','Tasks, microtasks, and event propagation',
 'Browser JavaScript finishes its current synchronous work before running queued promise reactions. Microtasks run before the next timer task in these examples. Event bubbling carries an event from its target through ancestors; target is the originating element while currentTarget is the listener attachment. preventDefault cancels a cancelable default action; stopPropagation affects propagation and does not itself cancel that action. Keep these distinct when reading form and delegated event handlers.',
 S('console.log("A"); Promise.resolve().~then~(()=>console.log("B")); console.log("C");','A\nC\nB','A\nB\nC|B\nA\nC','The fulfillment reaction waits until the synchronous statements finish.','catch|resolve'),
 S('setTimeout(()=>console.log("timer"),0); ~queueMicrotask~(()=>console.log("micro")); console.log("sync");','sync\nmicro\ntimer','sync\ntimer\nmicro|micro\nsync\ntimer','The current work finishes, then the microtask runs before the timer task.','setTimeout|requestAnimationFrame'),
 S('list.addEventListener("click",event=>{console.log(event.~currentTarget~===list);});','true','false|undefined','currentTarget is the node whose listener is currently running.','target|relatedTarget','Assume a child inside list was clicked: what is logged?'),
 S('form.addEventListener("submit",event=>event.~preventDefault()~);','Cancels the normal form submission','Stops all bubbling by itself|Validates every field','preventDefault cancels this default action without stopping propagation.','stopPropagation()|stopImmediatePropagation()','What action does this handler prevent?')),
 L('forms-data','FormData and browser input contracts',
 'Input.value is text even for type=number; valueAsNumber provides a numeric value or NaN. Successful form controls use name as their submitted key, not id. Disabled controls are omitted from FormData. Native constraints such as required improve browser feedback but cannot replace server validation. URLSearchParams encodes query data without concatenating raw values. Read these boundaries before assuming that a valid-looking form produces the intended request.',
 S('<input id="size" type="number" value="${n}">\n<script>console.log(typeof document.querySelector("#size").~value~);</script>','string','number|undefined','The value property is text; valueAsNumber performs numeric interpretation.','valueAsNumber|checked','What type is logged?'),
 S('<form id="f"><input name="project" value="${word}"></form>\n<script>console.log(new FormData(document.querySelector("#f")).get(~"project"~));</script>','${word}','null|f','FormData keys come from name attributes.','"f"|"value"','What is logged?'),
 S('<form id="f"><input name="project" value="${word}" ~disabled~></form>\n<script>console.log(new FormData(document.querySelector("#f")).has("project"));</script>','false','true|undefined','Disabled controls are excluded from the submitted form data.','readonly|required','What is logged?'),
 S('const query=new URLSearchParams({q:"a&b"}); console.log(query.~get("q")~);','a&b','a|q=a%26b','The API preserves the value while handling serialization separately.','toString()|get("b")')),
 L('browser-state','Origins, storage, and page lifecycle',
 'Browser APIs have origin and lifecycle boundaries. localStorage stores strings and persists beyond a page reload for the origin, subject to browser policy and possible exceptions. JSON serialization preserves selected data, not functions or arbitrary object identity. A module script is deferred by default; DOMContentLoaded signals that parsing and deferred scripts have completed, not that every image has loaded. Fetch HTTP failures and network failures take different paths, so inspect Response.ok before trusting a body.',
 S('localStorage.setItem("lesson_count",String(${n})); console.log(typeof localStorage.~getItem("lesson_count")~);','string','number|object','Storage returns a string or null; it does not restore a numeric type.','length|key(0)','Assume storage is available: what type is logged?'),
 S('const value=JSON.parse(JSON.stringify({name:"${word}",run(){}})); console.log(~typeof value.run~);','undefined','function|object','JSON serialization omits function-valued object properties.','typeof value.name|typeof value'),
 S('<script ~type="module"~ src="app.js"></script>','Runs with module scope and deferred execution by default','Blocks parsing like a classic script without defer|Shares top-level bindings as window properties','Module scripts have their own scope and wait for parsing by default.','type="text/javascript"|async','What loading behavior does this request?'),
 S('const response=await fetch("/data"); if(~!response.ok~) throw new Error("HTTP failure");','Rejects this path for an HTTP 404 response','Relies on fetch to reject every 404|Treats every response as success','fetch normally fulfills for HTTP error statuses; inspect ok explicitly.','response.ok|false','Assume the server responds 404: what does this check do?'))]
